A||||14||fierodave||No fast idle warm up/84 iron duke||||5||
Z||000000||fierodave||05-28-2000||03:38 AM||||<BR>Hi people!<BR> <BR>I have a 84 SE automatic 105,000 miles <BR>I just picked up,   It has this small problem! <BR>At start up it will idle at 1,000 rpms.   It has no fast idle at all,<BR>like my other 2.5's <BR>I have checked the codes, and it was giving me a code 33, I replaced the map sensor and reset the computer, and now have no codes stored at all! <BR>But I can't get it to have the fast warm up cycle at all! I pulled the IAC, cleaned the port and the pintle of the IAC,   but it does the same thing! I pulled a IAC from other 2.5 that is working right, and no change at all! <BR>I can adjust the pintle and change my idle so it won't choke off when put in gear, but that leaves me with a 1,500 rpm in neutral and a 1,000 rpm in drive! <BR>The car runs perfect aside from this minor problem! <BR>This will be my winter car and it would be nice to have the warm up cycle working! any help would be great! <BR>I did the pull the plug from the map while running and it did throw the code 34 like the book says. <BR>what connections do I check on the computer, or am i looking in the wrong place here? <BR>The Plug end for the IAC appears free of corrosion too! <BR>Thanks for the space<BR>FD||||reg||5
Z||000001||William Federle||05-28-2000||03:47 AM||||FD - Almost everyone who has posted about an Iron Duke has complained about a fast idle. I wish I had your problem. But I think your problem isn't as what you think it is.<P>I've noticed that my 84 SE (manual) idles slower since the weather has warmed up. Sometimes it goes about 2500 for 30 sec or so and then idles down to 1500. After I get home to Milwaukee from Fond du Lac, it idles about 1250. In other words, I think your low idle speed is at least partially due to the weather.<P>By the way, I know this isn't the mall, but since you're here, do you have an '87 radiator fan assem. and if so how much do you want for it?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 05-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierodave||05-28-2000||05:26 AM||||have to disagree with you!  sorry!<BR>but i have my Indy 4 popper sitting right next to it,  and when I start it(the Indy) it starts at 2,000 rpm for like 10 sec's then 1,500 rpm for 2 minutes then down to 1,000 rpms---- in stages<P>I have no stages with this 84 in question.  and it's been 40 here at night!  it should have the warm up cycle working.<P>email me with the details on the fan you need!  engine 2.5 -2.8 AC, GT, SE etc.<BR>FD||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||05-28-2000||07:23 AM||||try doing an ohm reading on your coolant temp sensor.<P>My 2.5 does idle high on warm ups.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-28-2000||07:53 AM||||I looked in my 86 service manual and it says to check the coolant temp sensor and the MAF sensor on the airfilter assembly. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||05-28-2000||08:15 AM||||I guess I tend to generalize too much. That's the good thing about this forum. It brings you back down to earth.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||05-28-2000||08:54 AM||||I absolutely hate that warm up cycle. I had never even heard of cars having these fast rpm warm up cycles until I bought the Fiero. Something about starting my Fiero in -20 weather and allowing the engine to immediatly punch up to 1,500rpm. Too bad it isn't something reasonably easy to get rid of, I would. I assume most all GMs from the 80's are programmed the same, but I think its stupid.||||reg||1
Z||000007||William Federle||05-28-2000||11:07 AM||||I didn't realize that the pattern you describe was normal. I guess my car's more normal than I thought. <P>If the computer is set up to idle that way, maybe the coolant temperature sensor (in the thermostat neck) is defective giving information to the computer that indicates that the engine is warm.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierodave||05-28-2000||05:25 PM||||(Orei I looked in my 86 service manual and it says to check the coolant temp sensor and the MAF sensor on the airfilter assembly)<P>The 2.5 4 cylinders do not have a MAF sensor.  they have a MAP, That was changed,  <BR>I also installed a new GM coolant sensor today!-------NO change!<BR>Got you all now  don't I ?  lol  <P>Who ever has the  diagram on the IAC's wires from the computer, please let me know which connection on the computer I check!  also  what is the way to test the IAC's funtions?<BR>FD<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||05-29-2000||12:31 AM||||A fixed idle. would have to be either wiring or the ECM since the IAC has been switched.<P>try this. take out the IAC and set it to a distance. Measure the distance. put it back in. run the car thru a normal IAC reset. (drive over 35 for at least 90 sec.)<P>if the idle is still off take out the IAC and measure it again. Is the number the same? if it is it's wiring or the ECM.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierospeeder||05-29-2000||06:44 AM||||fierodave, told you, you should have did an ohm test before buying one.  <P>Well, now your car is on its way to being brand new.||||reg||1
